<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chauri Chaura incident</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chauri Chaura incident took place on 4 February 1922 at Chauri Chaura in the Gorakhpur district of the United Provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. After police fired on a procession of protesters, the crowd set fire to the police station, killing 22 policemen. Shocked by the violence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> called off the nationwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/non-cooperation-movement">Non-cooperation movement</a>, and he was arrested shortly afterwards.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>From 1920 Indians led by Mohandas Gandhi had taken part in the nationwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/non-cooperation-movement">Non-cooperation movement</a>, organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> with the non-violent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-disobedience">civil disobedience</a> known as Satyagraha, against measures such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rowlatt-act">Rowlatt Act</a> and with Swaraj, home rule, as its goal. On 2 February 1922 volunteers led by Bhagwan Ahir, a retired soldier of the British Indian Army, protested against high food prices and liquor sales at Gauri Bazaar. The police under the local inspector, Gupteshwar Singh, beat them back and locked several leaders up at the Chauri Chaura police station.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 4 February some 2,000 to 2,500 protesters marched to picket the liquor shop at Gauri Bazaar, shouting anti-British slogans. Warning shots fired into the air only angered the crowd, which threw stones, and the sub-inspector Prithvi Pal ordered the police to fire, killing three and wounding others. The outnumbered police fell back to the chowki, the police station, which the crowd set alight. Most of those inside burned to death; some appear to have been killed at the entrance and thrown back into the fire. One constable, Raghuvir Singh, declared dead, was found alive months later and became the sole witness in court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The authorities declared martial law around Chauri Chaura and arrested hundreds. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> fasted for five days as penance, judging that he had urged revolt without preparing people for ahimsa, non-violence; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> and most Congress workers, then in prison, thought his halt hasty and wrong. Gandhi was sentenced to six years' imprisonment and released in February 1924 because of ill health.</p><p>Of 225 people tried at the Gorakhpur Sessions Court of Judge H. E. Holmes, 170 were sentenced on 9 January 1923 to hang, verdicts the communist leader M. N. Roy called legalised murder. On appeal the High Court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>, on 30 April 1923, confirmed 19 death sentences, and those men were hanged between 2 and 11 July 1923. A memorial to the dead policemen was raised in 1923; after independence local people and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> built memorials to the men who were hanged.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chauri Chaura incident” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Chauri Chaura incident</b> of February 1922, in which police fired on a procession and the crowd burned the police station, killing 22 policemen, Gandhi called the campaign off.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
